---
import Site from '../../layouts/Site.astro';

const sources = [
  { name: 'Aggarwal et al., “GEO: Generative Engine Optimization” (Princeton, Georgia Tech, Allen Institute for AI, IIT Delhi)', url: 'https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.09735' },
  { name: 'Ahrefs — AI Overviews and click-through rate', url: 'https://ahrefs.com/blog/' },
  { name: 'Seer Interactive — AI Overview citation impact', url: 'https://www.seerinteractive.com/insights' },
  { name: 'SE Ranking — llms.txt adoption across 300,000 domains', url: 'https://seranking.com/blog/' },
  { name: 'Cloudflare — crawler policy and Pay Per Use', url: 'https://blog.cloudflare.com/' },
];
const articleSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: 'GEO vs SEO: what actually changes for your content',
  datePublished: '2026-07-01',
  dateModified: '2026-07-01',
  author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'AI Page Audit' },
  publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'AI Page Audit' },
};
---
<Site
  schema={articleSchema}
  title="GEO vs SEO: what actually changes for your content — AI Page Audit"
  description="Which of your SEO work carries over to AI search, which of it doesn't, and the measurable evidence for both. An honest diff."
>
  <section class="pagehead">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="eyebrow">Strategy · 6 min read · Updated July 2026</span>
      <h1>GEO vs SEO: what actually changes for your content</h1>
      <p>Generative Engine Optimization isn't a rebrand of SEO. Here's the honest diff — including the parts of your existing work that still carry.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <main>
    <article class="post wrap">
      <div class="answerbox">
        <b>The short answer</b>
        <p><strong>Roughly half your SEO work carries over — the technical half. What doesn't carry is the assumption that ranking well means being visible.</strong> Only about 10% of the URLs ChatGPT cites rank in Google's top 10 for the same query, so a strong ranking position is a weak predictor of whether you appear in AI answers at all.</p>
      </div>

      <p>GEO (Generative Engine Optimization) and AEO (Answer Engine Optimization) name the same shift. GEO is the term the academic literature uses; AEO is the wider industry umbrella. Neither is a marketing rebrand of SEO, and the difference is measurable rather than philosophical.</p>

      <h2>What carries over</h2>

      <p>More than the "SEO is dead" crowd admits. These remain load-bearing:</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Crawl access.</strong> If a bot can't fetch the page, nothing downstream matters. The principle is unchanged; the surface area grew. There are now more crawlers to satisfy (GPTBot, OAI-SearchBot, ClaudeBot, PerplexityBot, Google-Extended) and more places to accidentally block them. Cloudflare now restricts AI crawlers by default in some configurations and has set a September 2026 deadline for AI firms to separate search bots from AI bots — so your edge config is now as decisive as your robots.txt.</li>
        <li><strong>Authority.</strong> Sources a model already treats as reliable get cited more. Off-page reputation still compounds; it just cashes out as citation frequency rather than rank.</li>
        <li><strong>Structured data.</strong> Schema was useful for rich results. It's more useful now — it hands an engine an unambiguous machine-readable summary instead of asking it to infer your price, rating or author from prose.</li>
        <li><strong>Genuine subject depth.</strong> Thin content failed at SEO and fails harder here, because there's nothing quotable in it.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What genuinely changes</h2>

      <p>Four things, in rough order of how much they'll cost you to ignore.</p>

      <h3>Rendering became decisive</h3>
      <p><strong>Googlebot executes JavaScript. The answer-engine crawlers do not.</strong> This asymmetry has no equivalent in classic SEO, where JS-rendering problems were a ranking delay rather than a wall. A client-rendered page can hold position one on Google and simultaneously be an empty shell to ChatGPT, Claude and Perplexity. There is no error, no warning, and no partial credit — a page that's 80% JavaScript-rendered doesn't get 80% of a citation, it gets none.</p>

      <h3>Extraction replaced ranking</h3>
      <p>You aren't competing for a position in a list; you're competing to be a sentence inside a paragraph the engine writes. Models retrieve <em>passages</em>, typically a section, stripped of surrounding context. A section that opens with a direct, self-contained answer gets lifted. The same fact hedged and buried three paragraphs down does not — regardless of how well the page as a whole is written.</p>

      <h3>The metric changed shape</h3>
      <p>Rank tracking assumes a stable, checkable position. AI answers are generated per query, vary between runs, and cite a handful of sources rather than ordering everything. The replacement metric is <strong>share of voice</strong>: across a defined prompt set, in what fraction of answers do you appear, and how does that compare to named competitors? You can't infer it from Search Console — it has to be measured by querying the engines directly.</p>

      <h3>Citations became the currency</h3>
      <p>This is the one with a controlled study behind it. Researchers from Princeton, Georgia Tech, the Allen Institute for AI and IIT Delhi tested which content changes increase citation by generative engines. Adding statistics, citing authoritative sources, and including direct quotations all produced meaningful lifts. The commercial stakes: Seer Interactive found cited brands earn roughly <strong>120% more clicks per impression</strong> than uncited brands on the same query — while AI Overviews correlate with a 58% CTR drop overall (Ahrefs, February 2026). The aggregate loss is real; the distribution between cited and uncited is where the difference is made.</p>

      <h2>What to ignore</h2>

      <p>One piece of received wisdom is worth actively skipping. <strong>llms.txt does not correlate with citation.</strong> SE Ranking analysed 300,000 domains, found 10.13% adoption, and tested whether the file predicted citation frequency — removing the variable improved their model's accuracy. The major crawlers skip it and read HTML directly. It is a comfortable task that feels like progress and isn't.</p>

      <h2>The practical playbook</h2>

      <ol>
        <li>Verify every AI crawler can actually reach and read your key pages — robots.txt <em>and</em> edge/WAF, plus a raw-HTML check for JS-rendered content.</li>
        <li>Open each section with a concise, quotable answer. Phrase headings as the questions people actually ask.</li>
        <li>Add FAQ blocks, comparison tables and schema so content is structurally easy to lift.</li>
        <li>Replace vague claims with numbers, and attribute them to named sources.</li>
        <li>Name entities explicitly — assume every section will be read in isolation, because it will be.</li>
        <li>Measure citation rate and share of voice in real engines, then close the specific gaps.</li>
      </ol>

      <p>AI Page Audit is built for the first and last of those: <a href="/check">check crawler access</a>, then <a href="/audit">audit and fix</a>.</p>

      <h2>Sources</h2>
      <ul class="srclist">
        {sources.map((s) => (
          <li><a href={s.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener nofollow">{s.name}</a></li>
        ))}
      </ul>

      <div class="post-cta">
        <a href="/check" class="btn-primary">Check a page free</a>
        <a href="/blog/what-is-aeo" class="btn-ghost">Start with the AEO primer</a>
      </div>
    </article>
  </main>

  <style>
    .post { max-width: 760px; }
    .post p, .post li { font-size: 16px; color: var(--ink-2); line-height: 1.75; }
    .post h2 { font-size: 24px; font-weight: 800; letter-spacing: -.02em; margin: 38px 0 12px; }
    .post h3 { font-size: 18.5px; font-weight: 800; letter-spacing: -.015em; margin: 26px 0 8px; color: var(--ink); }
    .post ul, .post ol { padding-left: 22px; display: grid; gap: 10px; }
    .post b, .post strong { color: var(--ink); }

    .answerbox { border-left: 3px solid var(--accent); background: var(--accent-soft); border-radius: 0 12px 12px 0; padding: 18px 22px; margin-bottom: 26px; }
    .answerbox b { display: block; font-size: 11px; font-weight: 800; letter-spacing: .06em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--accent); margin-bottom: 6px; }
    .answerbox p { margin: 0; }

    .srclist { display: grid; gap: 7px; }
    .srclist li { font-size: 14.5px; color: var(--muted); }

    .post-cta { display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-top: 36px; flex-wrap: wrap; }
  </style>
</Site>
